We convert a monolithic service into microservices to improve scalability, flexibility, maintainability, and deployment.
Here's a breakdown of the key reasons:

🔧 1. Scalability
Monolithic: You must scale the entire application even if only one part (e.g., payment or login) needs more resources.

Microservices: You can scale individual components independently based on demand.

🚀 2. Faster Deployment
Monolithic: A small change requires redeploying the entire app.

Microservices: You can deploy only the changed service, reducing downtime and risk.

💡 3. Improved Maintainability
Smaller services are easier to understand, test, and update.

Teams can work independently on different services without affecting each other.

🧩 4. Technology Flexibility
Each microservice can use the most suitable language, framework, or database.

For example, one service can use Java + MySQL, while another can use Python + MongoDB.

👥 5. Independent Team Ownership
Teams can own and manage services end-to-end (design, development, deployment).

Improves productivity and accountability.

🛡 6. Fault Isolation
If one service fails (e.g., email service), the rest of the system can still function.

In monoliths, one bug can crash the whole app.

🧪 7. Better Testing and CI/CD
Easier to write tests and perform continuous integration/deployment per service.

Leads to faster delivery and higher quality code.

⚖ 8. Compliance and Security
Sensitive services (e.g., payment) can have strict security rules without affecting other parts.

⚠️ When Not to Use Microservices:
For small or simple applications, microservices may add unnecessary complexity.

Overhead in managing inter-service communication and deployment.

